    {
      "role": "user",
      "content": "Hi, I'm studying for my phonetics exam and I'm a bit confused. Can you explain the difference between voiced and unvoiced alveolar fricatives? Like, what actually happens with the vocal cords for the /z/ and /s/ sounds?",
      "source": "evaluator"
    },
    {
      "role": "assistant",
      "content": "For the voiced alveolar fricative /z/, your vocal cords vibrate while air is forced through a constriction at the alveolar ridge, creating friction against the vocal fold activity. In contrast, for the unvoiced counterpart /s/, the vocal cords remain separate and do not vibrate, so you produce the sound solely by forcing airflow through the same narrow passage without vocal cord vibration. This fundamental difference in phonation explains why one sounds buzzy and the other sharp and voiceless.",
      "source": "target",
